If we allowed functions accepting arguments, we might end up with this code:

```tsx
function sum(x, y) {
  return x + y;
}

const onced = once(sum);
sum(1, 2) // 3
sum(2, 4) // 3
```

which is very awkward. So I thought we should restrict our function to functions which accept no arguments...

> If we allowed functions accepting arguments, we might end up with this code:
> 
> ```tsx
> function sum(x, y) {
>   return x + y;
> }
> 
> const onced = once(sum);
> sum(1, 2) // 3
> sum(2, 4) // 3
> ```
> 
> which is very awkward. So I thought we should restrict our function to functions which accept no arguments...

This function returns a new function, so a situation like the one above is unlikely to occur.

```javascript
  function sum(a: number, b: number) {
    return a + b;
  }
  const oncedSum = once(sum);

  console.log(sum(1, 2)); // 3
  console.log(sum(2, 4)); // 6

  console.log(oncedSum(1, 2)); // 3
  console.log(oncedSum(2, 4)); // 3
```
<img width="366" alt="Screenshot 2024-10-03 at 5 18 52 PM" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ab11032d-46d5-48ce-8be3-5243d599a37d">

The two functions are independent.

But since we need backward compatibility, the best option we can choose might be supporting both:

```tsx
export function once<F extends () => any>(func: F): F;
export function once<F extends (...args: any) => void>(func: F): F;
```
